es that are removable and whose removal does not cause any required symbol to be missing from the remaining nodes. Also, nodes that are not removable must be retained. So we would keep all nodes that are not removable, plus any removable nodes that are necessary to have a required symbol that is not already covered by non-removable nodes. Also, we might need to keep ancestors of required non-removable nodes? Actually, if a node is not removable, it must be retained. But if a node is removable, we can decide to remove it, but we must ensure that for each required symbol, there is at least one node in the retained set with that symbol. Also, the tree structure might imply that parent nodes are needed to connect? But the problem doesn't explicitly say that we must maintain connectivity. The output is just a set of node ids; we don't need to output any tree structure. So we can remove nodes as long as the required symbols are still present.\n\nBut consider the possibility of removing a node that is an ancestor of a required node? If we remove a node, we also remove its descendants? The problem statement doesn't say. Usually in tree pruning, removing a node also removes its subtree. But here the nodes are independent? The input has a parent field indicating tree structure. The function might need to consider that removing a node removes all its descendants because they become unreachable? But the output only lists node ids, not the tree. So we could decide to keep a descendant even if its ancestor is removed? That seems unlikely because the tree represents a hierarchy; if a parent is removed, its children cannot exist in the tree. So probably we need to ensure that the retained nodes form a valid subtree (i.e., if a node is retained, all its ancestors up to root must be retained). The root is always retained. So we must retain ancestors of any retained node. That is a typical constraint.\n\nThus the problem likely is: Given a rooted tree with nodes having removable flag and utility, and a set of required symbols, we need to select a subset of nodes to retain such that:\n- The root is retained (non-removable).\n- For any retained node, its parent is also retained (so the retained set is a rooted subtree containing root).\n- For each required symbol, at least one node with that symbol is retained.\n- Among all possible such subsets, we want to maximize the total utility of retained nodes? Or minimize? The fixtures don't show utilities clearly. Let's compute total utility if we retain all non-removable plus necessary removable nodes.\n\nIn case 0, non-removable nodes: root, n1, n3, n5. Required symbols: root and s1. s1 is already satisfied by n1 (non-removable). So we don't need any removable nodes. But we might want to keep some removable nodes if they add utility? Typically we would want to maximize utility, so we would keep all removable nodes that do not break constraints? But if we keep a removable node, we must keep its ancestors (which are already kept because ancestors are non-removable or also kept). But we might also be allowed to remove removable nodes to reduce something? The problem name \"SE-PE-01\" might stand for \"Symbol Elimination - Pruning Example 1\". Possibly it's about removing nodes that are not necessary.\n\nLet's think of a common optimization: \"maximum utility subtree covering required symbols\" where you can prune removable nodes. Since non-removable must stay, we have to keep them. Then for each required symbol that is not already covered by non-removable nodes, we must select at least one removable node that provides that symbol, and we must keep its ancestors (which may include other removable nodes). The goal could be to maximize total utility of retained nodes, which would encourage keeping all removable nodes as long as they don't cause any problem? But there is no penalty for keeping more nodes. So the optimal would be to keep everything, because utilities are positive (all >0). But the root utility